  "Idioms, adages and proverbs": ["Idioms and Sayings", "Some sayings don't mean exactly what the words say.", ["An idiom is a phrase with a special meaning: \"break a leg\" means good luck.", "Adages and proverbs give advice: \"Look before you leap.\"", "Use context clues to figure out the meaning."], "What does \"It's raining cats and dogs\" mean?", ["Animals aren't really falling.", "The saying describes heavy rain.", "It means it is raining very hard."], "If the words sound silly, it's probably an idiom."],
  "Progressive verb tenses": ["Progressive Tenses", "Progressive tenses show actions that keep going.", ["Present progressive: am/is/are + -ing (She is reading).", "Past progressive: was/were + -ing (She was reading).", "Future progressive: will be + -ing (She will be reading)."], "Change \"We play\" to the past progressive.", ["Past progressive uses was or were.", "\"We\" goes with were.", "We were playing."], "Look for the -ing verb and its helping verb."],
  "Helping verbs: can, may, must": ["Modal Helping Verbs", "Modal verbs show ability, permission or need.", ["can = able to (I can swim).", "may = permission or maybe (You may go. It may rain).", "must = need to (You must stop).", "Modals are followed by a plain verb."], "Which word shows permission: \"You ___ use my pencil.\"", ["Permission means being allowed.", "\"May\" shows permission.", "You may use my pencil."], "Swap in \"am able to,\" \"am allowed to\" or \"have to\" to test meaning."],
  "Order of adjectives": ["Order of Adjectives", "When we use several adjectives, they go in a usual order.", ["Opinion, size, age, shape, color, origin, material, purpose.", "\"A lovely little old red barn\", not \"a red old little lovely barn\".", "Usually two or three adjectives are plenty."], "Which is correct: \"a blue big ball\" or \"a big blue ball\"?", ["Size comes before color.", "\"Big\" is size; \"blue\" is color.", "a big blue ball."], "Read it aloud. The right order usually sounds right."],
  "Prepositional phrases": ["Prepositional Phrases", "A prepositional phrase starts with a preposition and ends with a noun.", ["Prepositions: in, on, under, over, with, after, during.", "The phrase tells where, when or how.", "\"under the bridge\", \"after lunch\"."], "Find the prepositional phrase: \"The cat slept on the warm rug.\"", ["Find the preposition: on.", "The phrase goes to the noun: on the warm rug.", "\"on the warm rug\" tells where."], "Ask \"where?\" or \"when?\" to find the phrase."],
  "Fragments and complete sentences": ["Complete Sentences", "A complete sentence has a subject and a verb and makes a full thought.", ["Subject = who or what.", "Predicate = what the subject does or is.", "A fragment is missing one of these.", "A run-on joins sentences with no correct punctuation."], "Is \"Running down the hall\" a sentence?", ["Who is running? We don't know.", "There's no subject.", "It's a fragment. Fix: \"Jay was running down the hall.\""], "Ask \"who did what?\" If you can't answer, it's a fragment."],
  "Fixing run-on sentences": ["Fixing Run-Ons", "A run-on smashes two sentences together. Separate or join them correctly.", ["Use a period to make two sentences.", "Or use a comma and a joining word: and, but, so.", "A comma alone is not enough."], "Fix: \"I was tired I went to bed.\"", ["There are two thoughts: I was tired. I went to bed.", "Join with \", so\": I was tired, so I went to bed."], "Read it out loud and listen for where you stop to breathe."],
