  "Visual elements in texts": ["Visual Elements", "Pictures, maps and charts add meaning to texts.", ["Photos show what something looks like.", "Maps show where things are.", "Charts and graphs show data.", "Ask: what does this add that the words don't?"], "Why would an article about volcanoes include a cutaway diagram?", ["A cutaway shows the inside of a volcano.", "It helps readers see the parts, like the magma chamber.", "It explains what words alone can't show."], "Read captions; they explain the picture."],
  "Drawing conclusions": ["Drawing Conclusions", "Put clues together to decide what's true.", ["Collect several details from the text.", "Think about what they add up to.", "Your conclusion should be supported by evidence."], "The ground is wet, people carry umbrellas, and puddles fill the street. What can you conclude?", ["Wet ground, umbrellas, puddles.", "All point to rain.", "It has been raining."], "One clue is a guess; several clues make a conclusion."],
  "Research and note-taking": ["Research and Notes", "Research means finding information and keeping track of where it came from.", ["Start with a focused question.", "Use reliable sources: experts, museums, government sites.", "Take notes in your own words (paraphrase).", "List your sources at the end."], "Which is the best research question?", ["Good questions are focused and can't be answered with yes or no.", "\"How do rainforest plants survive with little sunlight?\""], "Write the source next to every note so you can find it again."],
  "Informative writing": ["Informative Writing", "Teach your reader about a topic clearly.", ["Introduce the topic with a hook.", "Organize facts into paragraphs with headings if helpful.", "Use precise words and topic vocabulary.", "Link ideas with words like also, for example, in contrast."], "Which detail best supports \"Hummingbirds are amazing fliers\"?", ["Look for a fact about flying.", "\"Hummingbirds can fly backward and hover in place.\""], "End with a conclusion that sums up what you taught."],
  "Narrative techniques": ["Narrative Techniques", "Writers use special tools to make stories come alive.", ["Dialogue shows how characters think and feel.", "Description helps readers picture the scene.", "Pacing speeds up or slows down the action.", "Transitions show time passing: meanwhile, later that day."], "Which line uses dialogue to show a character is scared?", ["Look for a character speaking.", "\"W-what was that?\" Leo whispered.", "The stutter and whisper show fear."], "Let characters show their feelings instead of telling them."],
  "Citing text evidence": ["Citing Evidence", "Back up every answer with proof from the text.", ["Use phrases like \"The text states…\" or \"According to the author…\".", "Quote exact words in quotation marks.", "Explain how the evidence supports your answer."], "Why was the town proud? Cite evidence.", ["Find the detail: \"The town cheered as the new bridge opened.\"", "Explain: the cheering shows pride in the bridge."], "Answer, cite, explain: A-C-E."],
  "Central idea and summary": ["Central Idea and Summary", "The central idea is what a text is mostly about.", ["Look at the title, headings and first and last paragraphs.", "Key details support the central idea.", "A summary states the central idea and key details briefly."], "A text explains how recycling saves energy, reduces trash and protects animals. What is the central idea?", ["All the details are benefits of recycling.", "Central idea: recycling helps the planet in many ways."], "If a detail could be removed without changing the meaning, leave it out of the summary."],
  "Pronoun case": ["Pronoun Case", "Use the right form of a pronoun for its job.", ["Subject pronouns do the action: I, he, she, we, they.", "Object pronouns receive the action: me, him, her, us, them.", "Possessive pronouns show ownership: my, his, her, our, their."], "Choose: \"Mom took Sam and (I / me) to the zoo.\"", ["Cover \"Sam and\": Mom took ___ to the zoo.", "\"Mom took me\" sounds right.", "Mom took Sam and me to the zoo."], "Cover the other person's name to test the pronoun."],
